290
00:14:57,994 --> 00:15:00,694
Japanese were very reluctant to

291
00:15:00,694 --> 00:15:03,394
open their market to foreign insurers.

292
00:15:03,394 --> 00:15:07,134
So we had to force the market open.

293
00:15:07,134 --> 00:15:08,134
And we did.

294
00:15:08,134 --> 00:15:09,134
We used...

295
00:15:09,134 --> 00:15:13,494
The US government was very much on our side.

296
00:15:13,494 --> 00:15:16,334
And if the Japanese didn't open their market to us...

297
00:15:16,334 --> 00:15:17,334
We didn't.

298
00:15:17,334 --> 00:15:20,774
We worked hard to keep some of their

299
00:15:20,774 --> 00:15:24,214
own companies, whether it was insurance or otherwise, or

300
00:15:24,214 --> 00:15:27,534
other things, from doing business in the United States.

301
00:15:27,534 --> 00:15:30,434
We didn't hesitate to use the US government

302
00:15:30,434 --> 00:15:33,334
to support our desire to open markets around

303
00:15:33,334 --> 00:15:36,634
the world.

304
00:15:36,634 --> 00:15:41,084
When there was negotiation on trade, for example,

305
00:15:41,084 --> 00:15:45,534
most of the negotiation in the early years

306
00:15:45,534 --> 00:15:46,334
was on goods.

307
00:15:46,334 --> 00:15:47,334
Goods.

308
00:15:47,334 --> 00:15:48,334
Goods in trade.

309
00:15:48,334 --> 00:15:49,334
Things.

310
00:15:49,334 --> 00:15:50,334
Not financial services.

311
00:15:50,334 --> 00:15:55,184
Financial services were not negotiated at

312
00:15:55,184 --> 00:16:00,034
the World Trade Negotiations, the WTO.

313
00:16:00,034 --> 00:16:03,314
We brought that into being.

314
00:16:03,314 --> 00:16:05,384
I served on the President's

315
00:16:05,384 --> 00:16:07,454
Advisory Board for Trade Negotiations.

316
00:16:07,454 --> 00:16:13,334
They never heard of negotiating financial services.

317
00:16:13,334 --> 00:16:14,784
And it took us a while

318
00:16:14,784 --> 00:16:16,234
to get that done, but we did.

319
00:16:16,234 --> 00:16:19,054
We opened the market for

320
00:16:19,054 --> 00:16:21,874
financial services to be negotiated.

321
00:16:21,874 --> 00:16:25,664
And there are rules then that came into

322
00:16:25,664 --> 00:16:29,454
existence so that you can negotiate trade and services.

323
00:16:29,454 --> 00:16:31,514
It wasn't easy.

324
00:16:31,514 --> 00:16:34,124
Even when the rules were

325
00:16:34,124 --> 00:16:36,734
changed, countries dragged their feet.

326
00:16:36,734 --> 00:16:38,984
Took a long time to open,

327
00:16:38,984 --> 00:16:41,234
as I said, Japan, Korea, China.

328
00:16:41,234 --> 00:16:42,234
It took me from 1975.

329
00:16:42,234 --> 00:16:43,234
The first time I visited China.

330
00:16:43,234 --> 00:16:44,234
I was in China.

331
00:16:44,234 --> 00:16:45,234
I was in China.

332
00:16:45,234 --> 00:16:46,234
The first time I visited China.

333
00:16:46,234 --> 00:16:50,464
To 1992, to get the first life

334
00:16:50,464 --> 00:16:54,694
insurance license ever granted to a foreign company.

335
00:16:54,694 --> 00:16:58,324
Moreover, all other foreign companies afterwards could

336
00:16:58,324 --> 00:17:01,954
only get a license where they could

337
00:17:01,954 --> 00:17:04,114
only own 49%.

338
00:17:04,114 --> 00:17:06,114
We owned 100%.

339
00:17:06,114 --> 00:17:08,944
And today, still, it's the only foreign

340
00:17:08,944 --> 00:17:11,773
company in China, life insurance company, that owns

341
00:17:11,773 --> 00:17:12,874
100% of the company.

342
00:17:12,874 --> 00:17:13,874
So, it's a long way to go.

343
00:17:13,874 --> 00:17:14,874
But we're going to do it.

344
00:17:14,874 --> 00:17:14,914
We're going to do it.

345
00:17:15,233 --> 00:17:15,914
It wasn't easy.

346
00:17:15,914 --> 00:17:19,174
As I say, it took from 1975 to 1992.

347
00:17:19,174 --> 00:17:22,614
And I visited China every year, a couple

348
00:17:22,614 --> 00:17:26,054
of times a year to make that happen.

349
00:17:26,054 --> 00:17:27,854
But we did a lot of things for China.

350
00:17:27,854 --> 00:17:30,354
At the same time, we helped China.

351
00:17:30,354 --> 00:17:34,464
I lobbied very hard for China's entry into

352
00:17:34,464 --> 00:17:38,574
WTO, which was very important for our country

353
00:17:38,574 --> 00:17:41,734
and for China, and really for the world.

354
00:17:41,734 --> 00:17:43,234
So we were very active.

355
00:17:43,234 --> 00:17:44,234
And you had to be in foreign policy.

356
00:17:44,234 --> 00:17:45,234
And you had to be in foreign policy.

357
00:17:45,234 --> 00:17:48,794
So we had to be committed to foreign policy matters.

358
00:17:48,794 --> 00:17:50,994
It was linked to our business.

359
00:17:50,994 --> 00:17:54,144
Since we did business, as I said,

360
00:17:54,144 --> 00:17:57,294
ultimately in 130 countries, it became essential that

361
00:17:57,294 --> 00:17:59,714
we were at the leading edge

362
00:17:59,714 --> 00:18:02,134
of what the foreign policy issues were.

363
00:18:02,134 --> 00:18:05,804
We did business behind the Iron Curtain before

364
00:18:05,804 --> 00:18:09,474
the Iron Curtain came down, in Hungary, Poland,

365
00:18:09,474 --> 00:18:11,954
and Romania.

366
00:18:11,954 --> 00:18:15,074
Moreover, I went to the Soviet Union.

367
00:18:15,074 --> 00:18:18,864
during the height of the Cold War

368
00:18:18,864 --> 00:18:22,654
and began a reinsurance relationship with them.

369
00:18:24,194 --> 00:18:27,054
And that lasted throughout the years.

370
00:18:27,194 --> 00:18:29,364
Even during the height of the Cold

371
00:18:29,364 --> 00:18:31,534
War, we had a relationship going on.

372
00:18:31,594 --> 00:18:32,394
It wasn't easy.

373
00:18:34,194 --> 00:18:35,754
They thought I was in

374
00:18:35,754 --> 00:18:37,314
the CIA most of the time.

375
00:18:38,334 --> 00:18:42,994
It was a difficult thing, but we persisted.

376
00:18:42,994 --> 00:18:46,574
And when the Iron Curtain came down,

377
00:18:46,574 --> 00:18:50,154
we had a head start against anybody else.

378
00:18:51,794 --> 00:18:55,164
Doing business in Romania and Hungary and Poland

379
00:18:55,164 --> 00:18:58,534
before the curtain came down was not easy,

380
00:18:58,694 --> 00:19:02,454
but we built relationships so that when it did

381
00:19:02,454 --> 00:19:06,214
happen, we had a head start against anybody else.

382
00:19:06,914 --> 00:19:10,754
We operated all through Latin America, the Middle East.

383
00:19:12,114 --> 00:19:12,954
Parts of Africa.

384
00:19:13,114 --> 00:19:16,634
And all of those are a story in themselves,

385
00:19:16,634 --> 00:19:20,154
because in each country, it was a different environment,

386
00:19:20,874 --> 00:19:23,354
and you had to deal

387
00:19:23,354 --> 00:19:25,834
with different personalities and governments.

388
00:19:27,014 --> 00:19:28,254
It was very difficult.

389
00:19:29,254 --> 00:19:31,314
But we also had some basic principles.

390
00:19:31,474 --> 00:19:33,994
We would never, never be involved in a bribe.

391
00:19:35,454 --> 00:19:37,414
Anybody in our company that got involved

392
00:19:37,414 --> 00:19:39,374
in anything like that would be fired instantly.

393
00:19:40,594 --> 00:19:42,974
We understood what the foreign corrupt practice was.

394
00:19:43,094 --> 00:19:46,544
We knew what the Foreign Practices Act meant before

395
00:19:46,544 --> 00:19:49,994
they even had such a corrupt Foreign Practices Act.

396
00:19:49,994 --> 00:19:51,534
So we had a reputation that

397
00:19:51,534 --> 00:19:53,074
you didn't tamper with us that way.

398
00:19:53,074 --> 00:19:55,844
And they knew also that we wouldn't

399
00:19:55,844 --> 00:19:58,614
hesitate to retaliate through our government if necessary,

400
00:19:59,094 --> 00:20:01,514
if they tried to keep us out of their country.

401
00:20:02,334 --> 00:20:04,314
So we were tough on that basis.

402
00:20:04,314 --> 00:20:07,054
We wanted open markets, and we did.

403
00:20:07,914 --> 00:20:09,904
Now, you go back to what

404
00:20:09,904 --> 00:20:11,894
kind of a structure did we have?

405
00:20:11,974 --> 00:20:12,974
And how do you bring back the structure?

406
00:20:12,974 --> 00:20:17,384
How do you bring a group of people

407
00:20:17,384 --> 00:20:21,794
together that can work so harmoniously and enthusiastically together?

408
00:20:21,934 --> 00:20:22,594
It's critical.

409
00:20:23,154 --> 00:20:24,694
You're going to run a business like that.

410
00:20:25,174 --> 00:20:27,754
You have to have an organization

411
00:20:27,754 --> 00:20:30,334
that really everybody's on the same track,

412
00:20:30,974 --> 00:20:35,354
and everybody is enthusiastic about what they're doing.

413
00:20:36,674 --> 00:20:40,794
Our overseas people, we had what we called an MOP.

414
00:20:40,794 --> 00:20:42,914
It's Mobile Overseas.

415
00:20:42,974 --> 00:20:44,974
We had a lot of MOP personnel.

416
00:20:44,974 --> 00:20:46,974
It was like our own State Department.

417
00:20:46,974 --> 00:20:49,694
You can be working in Nigeria today

418
00:20:49,694 --> 00:20:52,414
as a manager, and then six months later,

419
00:20:52,414 --> 00:20:56,494
you might be in Singapore or some other country.

420
00:20:57,254 --> 00:20:58,614
So you have to be mobile.

421
00:20:58,614 --> 00:21:01,634
You have to be prepared to move and

422
00:21:01,634 --> 00:21:04,654
not be reluctant because of one thing or another.

423
00:21:05,194 --> 00:21:10,214
And becoming an MOP was a very high honor.

424
00:21:10,694 --> 00:21:12,734
We didn't put — everybody couldn't get that designation.

425
00:21:12,734 --> 00:21:12,834
We didn't put — everybody couldn't get that designation.

426
00:21:12,834 --> 00:21:13,934
We didn't put — everybody couldn't get that designation.

427
00:21:13,934 --> 00:21:15,354
You had to earn it.

428
00:21:15,354 --> 00:21:18,874
And it was a great group of people.

429
00:21:19,954 --> 00:21:22,974
But how do you motivate people to do that?

430
00:21:22,974 --> 00:21:25,174
How do you get an

431
00:21:25,174 --> 00:21:27,374
organization that can be so effective?

432
00:21:27,374 --> 00:21:33,484
AIG at its pinnacle, when I left

433
00:21:33,484 --> 00:21:39,594
in 2005, was by far the largest

434
00:21:39,594 --> 00:21:41,934
and most profitable insurance company in history.

435
00:21:41,934 --> 00:21:45,614
When you think about that, from a dead stop.

436
00:21:45,614 --> 00:21:49,444
Now, we had a compensation structure

437
00:21:49,444 --> 00:21:53,274
that was unique to the industry.

438
00:21:53,274 --> 00:21:55,534
I mentioned briefly to the professor.

439
00:21:56,774 --> 00:22:00,874
C.V. Starn Company was the — we considered

440
00:22:00,874 --> 00:22:04,974
to be the ultimate company in the group.

441
00:22:06,174 --> 00:22:09,374
It was the — it spawned AIG.

442
00:22:10,054 --> 00:22:10,964
And so within the organization, it

443
00:22:10,964 --> 00:22:11,874
was a very, very good organization.

444
00:22:11,874 --> 00:22:11,894
And it was the only organization that was successful,

445
00:22:11,894 --> 00:22:11,914
and it was the only organization that was successful.

446
00:22:11,914 --> 00:22:11,934
And it was the only organization that was successful.

447
00:22:11,934 --> 00:22:13,814
And it was the only organization that was

448
00:22:13,814 --> 00:22:15,694
viewed as the top company in the group.

449
00:22:16,794 --> 00:22:21,364
And since we spawned AIG and put

450
00:22:21,364 --> 00:22:25,934
assets into AIG in exchange for AIG stock,

451
00:22:26,694 --> 00:22:32,314
so that C.V. Starn and Starn International, the

452
00:22:32,314 --> 00:22:37,934
two companies, owned in the beginning 100% of AIG.

453
00:22:38,614 --> 00:22:40,264
As we grew and we needed

454
00:22:40,264 --> 00:22:41,914
more capital, we needed more capital.

455
00:22:41,914 --> 00:22:42,164
As we grew and we needed

456
00:22:42,164 --> 00:22:42,414
more capital, we needed more capital.

457
00:22:42,414 --> 00:22:44,343
As we were growing so

458
00:22:44,343 --> 00:22:46,273
rapidly, we had to raise capital.

459
00:22:46,273 --> 00:22:47,973
And therefore, we got somewhat diluted.

460
00:22:48,614 --> 00:22:51,433
But even at the end,

461
00:22:51,433 --> 00:22:54,253
we controlled about 15% of AIG.

462
00:22:54,253 --> 00:22:58,793
But the market cap was close to $200 billion from

463
00:22:58,793 --> 00:23:03,334
a dead stop of $300 million when we first went public.

464
00:23:03,334 --> 00:23:05,933
From $300 million to $200 billion, one

465
00:23:05,933 --> 00:23:08,533
of the largest companies in the world.

466
00:23:09,394 --> 00:23:11,694
So we weren't afraid of anybody taking us over.

467
00:23:11,694 --> 00:23:16,094
Nobody was big enough to even to attempt that.

468
00:23:17,033 --> 00:23:20,614
But what we did, we had several principles.

469
00:23:21,434 --> 00:23:24,734
Nobody could earn more than a million dollars in salary.

470
00:23:26,214 --> 00:23:27,374
I put that rule in.

471
00:23:28,273 --> 00:23:30,253
Two, nobody would have a contract.

472
00:23:31,114 --> 00:23:33,174
You stayed in AIG because you loved it

473
